fact WorkItem(id: "WI-792", description: some(value: "POSITIONAL ARGUMENT TYPES ARE NEVER CHECKED at a function-VALUE application, so a wrong-typed value flows with no trap and no diagnostic. MEASURED end-to-end on main: 'operation take(f: (x: Int64, y: Bool) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(true, 7)' with 'operation impl(x: Int64, y: Bool) -> Int64 = x' and 'operation drive() -> Int64 = take(impl)' LOADS CLEAN and drive() evaluates to Bool(true) -- a Bool returned from an operation declared -> Int64. Note there is NO permutation, NO subtyping and NO conformance question here: the call site simply hands Bool to the Int64 slot and Int64 to the Bool slot, and nothing objects. ROOT CAUSE: check_apply_expr Path 2 (kb/typing.rs ~9583, the callee-is-a-variable-of-arrow-type path) computes the result type and effects via extract_function_type_parts, which reads ONLY the arrow's  and  children and DISCARDS . It then merges argument effects and runs the WI-783 named-argument coverage + reorder -- which checks LABELS only, never the types behind them -- and returns. No positional argument is ever compared against the arrow's parameter list. Path 1's per-argument validation (validate_arg_against_param) is nested inside the  block and is NOT shared with Path 2. WHY THIS MATTERS BEYOND ITSELF: WI-782 tightened the arrow-vs-arrow CONFORMANCE relation (equal arity + positional slots) on the premise that a mis-aligned parameter list would then fail on the TYPES. That premise holds only where conformance is actually checked; this ticket shows the APPLICATION site enforces nothing, so any residual mis-alignment stays silent rather than trapping. It is also strictly more reachable than WI-782's own case 1, needing no permutation at all. Found by /code-review of the WI-782 commit; PRE-EXISTING (Path 2's param-discarding predates WI-782 and that commit did not touch it). ACCEPTANCE: a positional argument whose type does not conform to the corresponding parameter slot is REJECTED AT LOAD with a located diagnostic naming the argument position and both types; the repro above is driven end-to-end and refused; a matching call still loads and evaluates; the arity of a positional application is checked against the parameter list too (see WI-791 -- until the slot reports arity faithfully, only the equal-arity case may be checkable); full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-20T17:00:24Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-792", author: "claude", content: "ROOT-CAUSE text repair: three quoted identifiers were eaten by shell quoting when this ticket was filed, leaving blanks in the description. The sentence should read: extract_function_type_parts reads ONLY the arrow term children named result and effects, and DISCARDS the child named param. And: Path 1 per-argument validation (validate_arg_against_param) is nested inside the lookup_operation_info_full(...) block in check_apply_expr and is NOT shared with Path 2. Nothing else in the description was affected.", at: "2026-07-20T10:52:12Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-792", author: "claude", content: "A SECOND LOCUS of this ticket's family, folded in here rather than filed separately, and it is NOT the Path 2 the description already names. Path 2 is the APPLICATION site (calling a function VALUE). This one is the ARGUMENT-PASSING site: handing a callback to a TYPE-PARAMETERIZED operation. Different code, same shape of defect -- a conformance check that simply does not run.\n\nMEASURED end-to-end on the WI-791 commit AND, identically, on its parent 20e4eb77, so it is PRE-EXISTING and is not a hole in WI-791's arity child:\n\n  (a) operation apply2[T](f: (x: T, y: T) -- Int64, v: T, w: T) -- Int64 = f(v, w)\n      with operation get_a(t: (a: Int64, b: Int64)) -- Int64 = t.a\n      and  operation drive() -- Int64 = apply2(get_a, 7, 8)\n      LOADS CLEAN and traps ArityMismatch{expected: 1, got: 2} at eval.\n      The SAME program written NON-generically -- take2(f: (p: Int64, q: Int64) -- Int64) -- is correctly REFUSED at load by WI-791. Genericity is the whole difference.\n\n  (b) the dual: operation apply1[T](f: (x: T) -- Int64, v: T) -- Int64 = f(v)\n      with a TWO-parameter op and apply1(two, (7, 8)) also loads clean, and here it EVALUATES (to -1) because eval's spread_eta_args spreads one positional tuple argument across a multi-parameter op.\n\nROOT CAUSE: validate_arg_against_param (kb/typing.rs) opens with a groundness gate -- if either resolved_type_is_ground(actual) or resolved_type_is_ground(declared) is false it does NOT run types_compatible at all. A declared callback type (x: T, y: T) -- Int64 is non-ground while T is free, so the full relation, which is where WI-791's arity equality lives, is never reached. The non-ground path delegates to validate_arrow_param_result, which checks param and result COMPONENT-WISE and only where each component is itself ground (WI-469's discipline: a genuinely polymorphic component must not be rejected early).\n\nWHY THIS IS CHEAPLY FIXABLE AND WHERE: ARITY IS THE ONE COMPONENT THAT CHECK COULD ALWAYS DECIDE. It is a ground Const(Int) child no matter how polymorphic the param and result types are, so the groundness discipline that justifies deferring everything else does not apply to it. validate_arrow_param_result already decomposes both arrows and is already the designated home for \"what can be decided about a non-ground callback\". Adding the arity equality there is a few lines and needs no new machinery. Note also that arrow_arity returns None for a Function[A, B, E] (which states no arity and cannot -- see WI-791), so the WI-775 caveat that function guards in its own comment is respected for free: a Function side simply yields no arity and the check skips.\n\nTHE DECISION WHOEVER CLOSES IT MUST MAKE, and the reason WI-791 stopped short: adding that check also refuses case (b) above, which WORKS today. It works only because eval spreads a single POSITIONAL tuple argument; the name-keyed spelling of the same program traps. WI-791 already took exactly this trade at the CONFORMANCE rung -- it refuses take(f: ((Int64, Int64)) -- Int64) given a 2-parameter op, pinned as two_parameter_operation_is_refused_for_a_tuple_argument_arrow, on the grounds that letting a type relation depend on how a caller happens to build its tuple is incoherent, and that the spread convention stays reachable through Function[(A,B),R]. Taking the same trade here is consistent; taking a different one is not. Either way it should be a stated decision, not a side effect.\n\nALSO, NOW RESOLVED: this ticket's ACCEPTANCE says \"the arity of a positional application is checked against the parameter list too (see WI-791 -- until the slot reports arity faithfully, only the equal-arity case may be checkable)\". WI-791 HAS LANDED (7d674df1): the arrow carries an arity child, agreed_arrow_arity compares it, and arity 1 versus arity n are now distinct types. That caveat no longer binds -- a positional application can be arity-checked outright.\n\nPINNED: known_gap_generic_callback_arrow_is_not_conformance_checked in rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs asserts case (a)'s CURRENT wrong behaviour (loads, then traps) on purpose, so the gap stays visible in the suite. It fails when this is closed -- replace it with a load-rejection assertion.", at: "2026-07-20T13:06:27Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-792", author: "claude", content: "DESIGN DECISIONS SETTLED (user, this session). This ticket now has NO OPEN FORKS and is implementable as stated. Three questions were raised; two were decided, and the third was WITHDRAWN because it turned out not to be a question. Recorded here so a fresh session can execute without re-deriving any of it.\n\nDECISION 1 -- the one the description flags: REFUSE case (b). TAKEN.\nAdding arity equality to validate_arrow_param_result also refuses apply1[T](f: (x: T) -> Int64, v: T) given a TWO-parameter op, which loads and evaluates to -1 today. Refuse it. Rationale is WI-791's, unchanged: it took exactly this trade at the CONFORMANCE rung (two_parameter_operation_is_refused_for_a_tuple_argument_arrow) on the grounds that letting a type relation depend on how a caller happens to build its tuple is incoherent. NEW SUPPORTING FACT since the ticket was written: WI-784 has landed (eff8ed6a), so the tuple-spread convention is now reachable through Function[A, B] for BOTH operations and lambdas, in BOTH application forms -- pinned by operations_and_lambdas_are_interchangeable_in_both_application_forms. Nothing is lost by refusing case (b); the working spelling remains available.\n\nDECISION 2 -- NOT IN THE DESCRIPTION, and it CONSTRAINS A GREEN TEST ON MAIN. The arity check on a POSITIONAL APPLICATION must SKIP when the callee states no arity.\narrow_declared_param_list returns None for a Function[A, B, E] -- WI-775 settled that A is the ARGUMENT's data type, ONE tuple-typed argument, not a parameter list -- but arrow_parts still DECOMPOSES a Function, so its param reads as the single type A. A naive \"positional argument i against parameter slot i\" check therefore sees ONE parameter at a Function slot and would REFUSE:\n\n  operation spread(f: Function[A = (Int64, Int64), B = Int64]) -> Int64 = f(3, 10)\n\nThat is 2 arguments against 1 parameter. It is a PATH 2 SITE (the callee f is a variable), it is GREEN on main, and it is pinned TWICE: operations_and_lambdas_are_interchangeable_in_both_application_forms (wi784_closure_arity_test.rs) and WI-791's function_spelling_states_no_arity_and_still_bridges. Locus 2 gets this behaviour for free, because arrow_arity already returns None for a Function. LOCUS 1 MUST DO IT ON PURPOSE.\n\nDECISION 3 -- RAISED AND THEN WITHDRAWN. It is not a fork. I had asked \"does a function-VALUE call inherit Path 1's Option auto-coercion (WrapSome), or do the two call paths coerce differently?\" That framing was wrong, and the codebase has already answered it. validate_arg_against_param's own doc says it is \"the shared core of the operation-argument (check_apply_iter) and entity-field (check_constructor_iter) validation\", and that some-coercion (WI-408) applies at \"both positions\". So it is a GENERAL CALL-SIDE TRANSFORMATION already applied at every argument position that exists; a function-value application is simply a THIRD such position that was never wired up -- the same shape as this ticket's own defect. There is no principled reason for it to differ.\n\nSo Decision 3 reduces to an implementation obligation: Path 2 becomes the third consumer and must ACT on WrapSome -- rewrite the argument occurrence -- copying either check_apply_iter (kb/typing.rs:8364 and :8392) or check_constructor_iter (:18161 and :18177). synthesize_some_wrap already takes exactly what Path 2 has (the child occurrence plus the declared type). THE ONE GENUINELY WRONG OPTION, to be avoided explicitly: treating WrapSome as Ok WITHOUT wrapping. That leaves the value bare in memory while its type says Option[T] -- the doc names it as precisely the behaviour WI-408 REPLACED (WI-385's lenient-accept interim). It is a known-bad state with a name, not a defensible shortcut.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION DIRECTION -- SHARE THE CORE, DO NOT REIMPLEMENT. Path 2 must CALL validate_arg_against_param rather than grow its own positional type check. A bespoke check would get the TYPES right and then silently lose the WI-408 some-coercion, the is_reflect_term_type reflection bypass, and the WI-385/WI-469 groundness discipline -- a THIRD divergent definition of \"does this argument fit this parameter\". This is the WI-784 lesson repeating verbatim: its /code-review caught a first cut that had DUPLICATED the binder-arity notion (the typer's lambda_written_arity beside a new closure_param_arity) instead of sharing it, and the fix was one shared Pattern::binder_arity read by both sides. Note Path 1's call sits INSIDE the lookup_operation_info_full(...) block in check_apply_expr and needs hoisting before Path 2 can reach it.\n\nSCOPE CORRECTION. An earlier statement of mine -- that locus 1 (the Path 2 positional check) is \"decision-free\" and could be split off as a quick win -- was WRONG: decisions 2 and 3 BOTH live in locus 1. What is true is that the two loci are INDEPENDENT OF EACH OTHER, so either may land first; neither is free of design content. Locus 1 closes the headline soundness hole (a Bool flowing into an Int64 slot, drive() returning Bool(true) from an operation declared -> Int64); locus 2 closes the generic-callback gap.\n\nTESTS TO WATCH.\n  MUST FLIP (asserts current wrong behaviour on purpose):\n    known_gap_generic_callback_arrow_is_not_conformance_checked (wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs) -- replace with a load-rejection assertion when locus 2 closes.\n  MUST KEEP PASSING (they encode decisions 1 and 2):\n    operations_and_lambdas_are_interchangeable_in_both_application_forms (wi784_closure_arity_test.rs)\n    caller_built_tuple_still_matches (wi784_closure_arity_test.rs)\n    function_spelling_states_no_arity_and_still_bridges (wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs)\n    two_parameter_operation_is_refused_for_a_tuple_argument_arrow (wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs)\n\nNOT FIXED BY THIS TICKET, so do not expect it to move: a 2-binder LAMBDA into a generic arity-2 callback slot was never a conformance gap -- the arity-2 lambda genuinely MATCHES the arity-2 slot. That case was WI-784's (the closure arm), and it is already closed.", at: "2026-07-20T15:57:15Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-792", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Both loci landed; full workspace green (3192 passed, 0 failed) and BLAST RADIUS WAS ZERO -- no pre-existing test changed except the one this ticket was told to flip. The arity tightening (Decision 1) and the argument type check refused only buggy programs, the same result WI-782's measurement method got.\n\nPRE-FIX MEASURED MYSELF on eff8ed6a in an isolated worktree, not taken from the ticket text. All four claims reproduce, and one is NEW:\n  * headline: LOADS CLEAN, drive() -> Ok(Bool(true)) from an op declared -> Int64. Confirmed verbatim.\n  * arity: BOTH f(1,2,3) and f(1) on a 2-parameter arrow LOADED CLEAN.\n  * NAMED channel had the IDENTICAL silent wrong answer: f(y: 7, x: true) also loaded and returned Bool(true). The ticket only described the positional channel; the labels were resolved by WI-783 and then what landed in the slot was never checked. Both channels are closed.\n  * NEW, and it sharpens Decision 3: the Option case was not merely unchecked, it was load-clean-then-RAISE. take(f) = f(7, 3) against (o: Option[T=Int64], d: Int64) loaded and drive() RAISED on the failed match, because the bare 7 was never wrapped. So the known-bad shortcut (treat WrapSome as Ok without wrapping) is indistinguishable from the fix at LOAD time -- only driving separates them. The test drives it.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION, as directed: locus 1 CALLS validate_arg_against_param rather than growing its own check, so the WI-408 some-coercion, the is_reflect_term_type bypass and the WI-385/WI-469 groundness discipline all come with it; Path 1's call did NOT need hoisting after all -- the function is a free fn already, only the callee's parameter list had to be read on the Path 2 side. Locus 2 is the predicted few lines in validate_arrow_param_result, ahead of the groundness-gated component checks.\n\nDECISION 2 needed one new reader, not a reuse of arrow_declared_param_list: that one returns None at ARITY 1 (the arrow drops a lone binder's name, so a LABEL has nothing to bind to), but a POSITIONAL argument needs no name and IS checkable there. New arrow_positional_param_slots differs from it in exactly that one arm and DELEGATES to it otherwise, so the two cannot drift. Function still yields None and skips, on purpose.\n\nMEASURED SIDE EFFECTS, both judged acceptable and neither breaking a test:\n  * an exact-arity check FORBIDS PARTIAL APPLICATION at a function-VALUE site. Checked before accepting: eval's spread_eta_args already refused any count but an exact one, so it was never executable -- this converts a run-time trap into a load error. WI-374 partial application is about NAMED-OPERATION calls (Path 1), untouched.\n  * the arity error now PRE-EMPTS WI-783's 'records no parameter names' message when a labelled call to a 1-parameter arrow ALSO has the wrong count. WI-783's message still fires when the count is right (verified with f(v: 1)).\n\n/code-review (high) found 6, 3 fixed: (1) arrow_positional_param_slots returned None for a MALFORMED arrow, conflating it with the legitimate Function absence -- that would SILENTLY DISABLE the whole check, the exact WI-791 footgun where a hand-built arrow missing a child left two tests green covering nothing; now a debug_assert, and arity 0 was measured to land on the empty-named_tuple arm first so it cannot false-fire. (2) a comment claimed a coverage-rejected label draws only one diagnostic -- FALSE for a DUPLICATE label, which does match a param and is type-checked too; measured and the comment corrected (behavior matches Path 1, left alone). (3) the parameter-list decomposition was duplicated across two readers; now delegated.", at: "2026-07-20T17:00:18Z")
